So if no one else arrived during our work, `A == c` (just us), so new `contenders = 0`, loop exits. 

If someone else arrived (called `consume` and incremented `contenders` atomic by 1, then returned early because pos > 0), then `A == c + k` where k is the number of new arrivals. New local `contenders = k > 0`, so we loop again and process any newly-ready jobs. This is the "re-entry" — we keep processing until no new contender signaled interest.

This ensures: while we're in the critical section, if new jobs are pushed and someone calls `consume`, that someone registers their interest (increments atomic) and bails out, leaving the current consumer to pick up the new work on the next iteration. This way, the critical section is held by exactly one thread at a time (mutual exclusion), but the holder keeps draining as long as there's work + interest.

The mutual exclusion: only the thread that sees `pos == 0` enters. All others return immediately. The atomic counter acts as both a lock (pos==0 is the "acquired" state) and a fairness signal (new arrivals keep the holder alive).

